.\" $Revision: 1.20 $
.TH MDINDEX 1 "2026-10-20" "mdindex 1.33" "Password Recovery"
.SH NAME
mdindex \- index very large wordlists by hash, and look words up by digest
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mdindex
.RB [ \-d
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR threads ]
.RB [ \-C
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-H
.IR types ]
.RB [ \-i
.IR depth ]
.RB [ \-vy ]
.B build
.I file
.RI [ file " ...]"
.br
.B mdindex
.RB [ \-d
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR threads ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.B update
.RI [ newfile " ...]"
.br
.B mdindex
.RB [ \-d
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ lookup ]
.RI [ hash " ...]"
.br
.B mdindex
.RB [ \-d
.IR dir ]
.B info
.br
.B mdindex
.RB [ \-d
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR threads ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.B compact
.br
.B mdindex
.RB [ \-d
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.B check
.RI [ index | overlay | all ]
.br
.B mdindex selftest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mdindex
builds a database mapping the digest of every distinct word in a set of
wordlists back to the file and byte offset where that word is stored, for
several unsalted hash types at once, and then answers lookups against it.
Given a hash it prints the word.  The wordlists are not copied; the index
holds only digests and positions, and the sources must stay where they were.
.PP
Output is the
.BR mdxfind (1)
shape,
.I TYPE hash:plain,
so a line pipes straight into
.BR hashpipe (1):
.PP
.RS
.nf
MD5x01 5f4dcc3b5aa765d61d8327deb882cf99:password
.fi
.RE
.SS The index is deliberately lossy
A record stores 96 bits of the digest, not all of it, and does not record
which hash function produced it.  Both are recovered rather than stored: a
hit is answered by reading the line, regenerating every configured digest of
it, and comparing against the whole hash you supplied.
.PP
So the index is only a filter, and the verification is exact.  A key
collision costs one wasted read and prints nothing \(em it cannot produce a
wrong answer.  This is also why adding a hash type costs a table entry and a
rebuild rather than a format change.
.SS Partial hashes
Because the supplied hash is checked in full after the line is read, a
.I partial
hash is a perfectly good query.  A query of
.I q
bits selects a contiguous run of buckets and, inside them, a contiguous run
of records, so you decide the cost by how many digits you have \(em the
counts below are against the 15.1-billion-record index described in
.BR info :
.PP
.RS
.nf
24 hex characters  exactly the stored key, about one candidate
16 hex characters  about one candidate
12 hex characters  about one candidate
 6 hex characters  one bucket, about 900
 4 hex characters  256 buckets, about 230,000
 2 hex characters  a 256th of the index, about 59 million
 1 hex character   a 16th of the index, about 943 million
.fi
.RE
.PP
The last two lines are why a very short query is accepted only as an argument
or from a terminal: each candidate costs a seek into a source file, so one
stray one-character line in a piped list asks for more reads than the rest of
the list together, and the run looks like a hang.
.PP
Every candidate is regenerated and compared against all the digits you
supplied, and
.B every match is printed,
with the full digest rather than the fragment you asked with.  A short query
against a multi-type index therefore returns hits of several different types,
which is correct: those digits really are the start of each of those digests.
.SS Hash types
Six types are indexed by default, described below.  They are not the limit:
.B \-H
selects from
.BR mdxfind 's
catalog, and a selected type is computed by the
.B hx
expression language \(em the same engine
.B mdxfind
and
.BR hashpipe (1)
use \(em rather than by a second implementation here, so a type cannot
compute one digest in this tool and another in those.
.PP
A type is indexable only if its digest is a function of the password
.IR alone .
.BR mdxfind 's
own type table declares what each type needs supplied, and anything wanting
a salt, a userid, a pepper or the structured-load channel is refused: the
digest stops being a function of the word, and salts arrive with the hashes
at crack time, long after the build.  That rule is a predicate over
.BR mdxfind 's
table rather than a list kept here, so a type whose flags are corrected
becomes indexable with nothing to edit.  Of 1,061 types, 620 qualify, and 551 of
those additionally carry an expression this build can compile, prove, and
tell apart from a type it already holds;
those are what
.B \-H all
selects.  The shortfall is the catalog's outlier rows, which carry markup
instead of an expression; the families that emit several digests per word;
and those that differ from a selected type only in output form, which is
no difference once the digest is stored as bytes.
.PP
A type that enumerates its own values IS indexable, because its digests
remain a function of the password alone.  Two families do.  The HEXSALT
family walks all 256 two-hex salts over three shapes, so one word yields
768 digests.  The seven HUM types walk eight line-ending separators over
two placements, after and before the hex of an inner digest, for 16.
Both enumerations are declared in the catalog rather than inferred, and
the matching member is reported in the output line as
.IR hash : salt : plain ,
because nothing could re-verify the line without it.  A salt that must be
SUPPLIED is still refused \(em it is not known at build time and never
will be.
.PP
Each selected type is then proved before a record is written: it must
produce a fixed-width, hexadecimal digest that changes with the password.
That rejects a stored form such as MySQL's starred SQL5 display string, and
a parameterised entry whose width depends on an unbound argument.  A type
named explicitly that fails is a fatal error; under
.B \-H all
it is skipped.  A type that will not compile at all is always fatal, on
build and on update alike \(em computing a different digest under a name the
index already holds would corrupt it silently.
.PP
.B MD5x01
is
.IR md5(pass) .
.B MD5x02
is
.IR md5(md5(pass)) ,
where the inner digest is consumed as its 32-character lowercase hex, which
is what
.B mdxfind \-i 2
computes.
.B SHA256x01
is
.IR sha256(pass) .
.B NTLMx01
is
.IR md4(utf16le(pass)) .
.B SQL5x01
is
.IR sha1(sha1_bin(pass)) ,
the MySQL 4.1+ password hash and
.BR hashcat (1)
mode 300.  Note the contrast with
.BR MD5x02 :
SQL5 consumes sha1's 20
.I raw bytes
where MD5x02 consumes md5's 32 hex characters.  MySQL displays the hash with
a leading
.BR * ,
which is not part of the digest;
.B mdxfind
will not load that form and neither will this \(em strip it first.
.B SHA1x01
is
.IR sha1(pass) ,
.BR hashcat (1)
mode 100.
.PP
NTLM is read up to three ways, as
.B mdxfind
reads it: Windows mapped the user's code page into UTF-16LE through its own
tables rather than zero-extending, so a word is indexed as UTF-8, as CP1251
and as CP1252, using
.BR mdxfind 's
own conversions.  An all-ASCII word collapses to one reading; a word that is
not well-formed UTF-8 loses the UTF-8 reading and keeps the other two.  For
the code-page readings the reported plaintext is the UTF-8 spelling of the
codepoints that were actually hashed, which is
.I not
the bytes in the wordlist \(em the same thing
.B mdxfind
reports, and the only form that re-verifies.
.SS Encoding wrappers
.BI $HEX[ ... ]
and
.BI $TESTVEC[ HH " x " N ]
are ways of writing a word, not decoration on one, so the digest indexed for
a wrapped line is the digest of the bytes the line
.I denotes,
never of its literal text.  A malformed wrapper is not an error: it is
hashed literally, exactly as
.BR mdxfind (1)
and
.BR hashpipe (1)
treat it.  On output a line stored wrapped is reported wrapped, because that
is the spelling the file holds and it already round-trips.
.SS Priority and duplicates
A digest is stored once.  When the same word appears in more than one source
file, the record kept is the one from the file that came
.B earliest on the build command line.
That order is recorded in the manifest and every later
.B update
honours it; within a file the first occurrence wins.  A word re-appearing in
an updated file adds nothing to the index.
.SH COMMANDS
With no arguments at all,
.B lookup
is assumed and hashes are read from standard input.  A bare hash in place of
a command word is also a lookup.
.TP
.BI build " file " ...
Build a new index from scratch, discarding any existing one.  Arguments are
in priority order.
.TP
.BI update " [newfile ...]"
Re-read each indexed file from the byte offset where the last build or update
stopped, and hash only what was appended.  A file named on the
.B update
line is
.I added,
at the end of the manifest and so at the lowest priority.
.IP
.B update
does not rewrite
.IR index.rec .
New records go to
.IR add.rec ,
an overlay beside it that
.B lookup
searches as well, so absorbing a day of new words costs those words and not
the index they join \(em the measured case was 164 new records against a
452 GB rewrite.  A key the main index already holds is dropped rather than
written, so the overlay tracks new words and not lines read.  See
.BR compact .
.IP
Sources may only GROW.  Before reading, each is checked against the size,
mtime and the MD5s of the first and last megabyte of the indexed region
recorded in the manifest.  Appending changes neither fingerprint; truncating,
rewriting or reordering changes at least one, and is reported as an ALERT
naming the file and what moved.  Reordering a wordlist invalidates every
offset into it and forces a full
.BR build .
.TP
.B compact
Fold the overlay into
.IR index.rec ,
through the same two-way merge
.B build
uses, and remove
.IR add.rec .
This is the only command that rewrites the main index, and it reads and
writes all of it, so it costs twice the index in I/O however small the
overlay is.
.B Nothing needs compacting to be correct:
a lookup searches both files and gives the same answer either way.  Run it
when
.B update
says the overlay is getting large, or whenever it suits.
.TP
.BI check " [index|overlay|all]"
Read every record and verify that each bucket is strictly increasing on the
stored key.  A descending pair is a sort failure; an equal pair is a
duplicate that dedup should have dropped.  Both have one cause, since dedup
only ever compares adjacent records.
.IP
.B check index
is a full pass over
.I index.rec
and costs an index-sized sequential read.
.B check overlay
reads only
.I add.rec
and is instant; that is the one worth running after an
.BR update ,
which cannot have changed
.IR index.rec .
The default is both.  Exit 2 means a fault was found.
.TP
.BI lookup " [hash ...]"
Print
.I TYPE hash:plain
for every match.  With no argument, or with
.BR \- ,
hashes are read from standard input, one per line, case-insensitive hex.
Nothing is printed for a hash that is absent.  Counts and timings go to standard error, so standard output carries
solutions or nothing and is safe to pipe.
.IP
From a pipe or a file, a line that is not hex, or whose length is outside the
range of digest sizes this database holds, is skipped without a word and only
counted \(em the total is printed after the summary.  A mixed file carrying
salts, plaintexts or blank lines can therefore be fed in as it stands.  Note
what this excludes: a short
.I hex
fragment, which from a pipe is almost always a plaintext that happens to look
like hex rather than a query anyone meant.
.IP
A hash given as an argument, or typed at a terminal, is taken exactly as
asked, however short, and a malformed one is named on standard error.  That
is where a deliberate partial query belongs.
.IP
A batch from a pipe or a file is read whole, sorted and answered together; a
terminal is answered a line at a time.  Sorting is what lets a large batch be
answered by one sequential pass over the index instead of one random read per
hash, which
.B mdindex
switches to automatically above roughly
.I records * 15 * 340 / 299e6
queries \(em about 257,000 against the full-size index above.  See
.B \-v.
.TP
.B info
Print the record and bucket counts, the key width, the hash types the
database was built with, and the manifest.  Also runs the source checks and
reports any ALERT.
.TP
.B selftest
Check every digest against a published vector or against what
.B mdxfind
prints, check both wrappers, then build a small index in a temporary
directory and check priority, partial lookup, the NTLM readings and
staleness detection end to end.  Touches nothing else.
.SS Reading hashes in
A query line may carry a non-hex lead-in.  A
.BR john (1)
hash names its format in front of the digest, and sometimes a login in
front of that:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$dynamic_0$0cc175b9c0f1b6a831c399e269772661
alice:$dynamic_0$0cc175b9c0f1b6a831c399e269772661
.fi
.RE
.PP
The digest begins after the second
.B $
in every such form, whatever precedes the first, so that is where
.B mdindex
resumes.  It does so only when the lead-in was the reason the line did
not parse, so a line that already read as a digest is never re-read as
something else.
.PP
A
.I trailing
field is left attached on purpose.  In a John line it is the salt, and a
salted digest is not something this index holds; leaving it makes the
line fail the hex test and be counted among the ignored, rather than
looked up as though it were unsalted and reported as an ordinary miss.
.SS Digests narrower than the key
A type whose digest is shorter than the 12-byte index key cannot be
indexed and is refused, whether it is named explicitly or reached through
.BR "\-H all" .
The scatter copies 12 bytes out of every digest, so a shorter one is read
past, into whatever happened to follow it in memory: the keys would not
reproduce between builds and the type would simply stop being found after
a rebuild, with nothing anywhere saying why.
.PP
Three catalog types fall under this \(em
.BR MYSQL3 ,
.B MURMUR64A
and
.BR MURMUR64AZERO ,
all eight bytes.  That width is also why they would match things they are
not: 64 bits of digest against an index keyed on 96.
.PP
A type that NESTS one of them is unaffected and is indexed, because
.B md5(mysql3(pass))
is an ordinary 16-byte MD5.  The primitives are available; only the bare
forms are refused.
.SS Build-cost projection
Before a
.B build
writes anything it projects what the build will cost, and refuses if that
will not fit:
.PP
.RS
.nf
projection  10,001 lines x 4892.09 records/line = 48,925,829 records
            scratch 839.9 MB, new index 699.9 MB, peak 1.5 GB; 1012.0 MB free
.fi
.RE
.PP
Lines are counted with the same SIMD newline scan
.BR mdxfind (1)
uses, over the unindexed tail of each source rather than the whole file,
and the count is looked up in and written to mdxfind's own line cache when
.B MDXFIND_CACHE
is set, keyed on path, size and mtime \(em so the pass usually costs
nothing, and a file that has grown misses rather than returning a stale
count.  Records per line is
.I observed,
by hashing a sample of the real input through the selected types, because
the ratio depends on how many lines are non-ASCII and on whether an
enumerated type is selected: the default six give 6.00 and the full
catalog at depth 2 gives 4,892.
.PP
Peak is scratch plus the new index plus the old one, which survives until
the rename.  If it exceeds
.I a tenth
of the free space on the database filesystem,
.B build
prints the projection, writes nothing and exits 1;
.B \-y
proceeds.  A tenth rather than a half because a build runs for hours while
other things write to the same filesystem, and the free-space reading is a
moment in time and not a reservation.
.PP
This is the one place
.B mdindex
refuses rather than reports.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-d " dir"
Database directory.  The default is
.IR /usr/local/share/mdfind .
.TP
.BI \-t " threads"
Worker threads for build and update.  The default is one per online core.
.TP
.BI \-C " file"
Read user-defined hash types from
.IR file ,
a stanza file in the format
.BR mdxfind (1)
reads as
.IR userdef.txt :
a
.BI [ name ]
header, an
.B id
that
.B \-H
selects the type by, and an
.B hx
expression taken verbatim to end of line.
.RS
.IP
.nf
[Cust1]
id = 47
hx = sha1(md5(pass)."register")
.fi
.RE
.IP
The file is named outright.  There is no search path, no default location
and
.B MDXFIND_CACHE
is not consulted, because this file decides what an index
.IR means :
a type selected from it is built into the index under its own name, and a
different file answering to that name later would reinterpret records
already on disk.  For the same reason the expression is copied into the
manifest, so
.B \-C
is needed to
.B build
and never to read: lookup, update, info and compact all take their types
from the manifest, and a
.B \-C
file that has since changed or been deleted cannot alter what the database
holds.
.IP
A user-defined type is indexable on the same terms as a catalog one: it
must be a function of the password alone.  An expression naming
.BR salt ,
.B salt2
.RB ( sic ),
.B userid
or
.B pepper
is refused, and here the refusal is permanent rather than provisional \(em
the catalog's HEXSALT family supplies its own salt from an
.B mdxfind
arm that enumerates it, and a user type has no such arm.
.IP
Iteration differs too.  A catalog type repeats its
.I outermost
call over the previous round's digest; a user type has no
.B mdxfind
arm, so
.BR mdxfind 's
generic loop re-runs the
.I whole
expression with
.B pass
bound to the previous digest, and
.B mdindex
builds that form.  A user expression that never names
.B pass
or that carries a prelude of its own is left at depth 1.
.TP
.BI \-H " types"
Comma-separated hash types to index.  Build only; the set is recorded in the
manifest.  A type is named in one of six ways, disjoint by grammar so that
nothing has to be tried in turn:
.RS
.IP "e\fIN\fP" 16
an
.B mdxfind
catalog type, as
.BR e259 .
.IP "u\fIN\fP" 16
a user-defined type from the
.B \-C
file, named by its
.B id
as
.BR u47 .
.IP "\fIN\fP" 16
a
.BR hashcat (1)
mode, as
.BR 300 .
.IP "dynamic_\fIN\fP" 16
a
.BR john (1)
built-in dynamic format, as
.BR dynamic_2 .
.IP "\fBall\fP" 16
every indexable catalog type.
.IP "anything else" 16
a canonical type name, as
.BR SHA1MD5PASS .
.RE
.IP
A trailing
.BI : N
selects
.BR mdxfind 's
iteration
.IR N ;
the default is 1.  A John label already names one \(em
.B dynamic_2
is
.B MD5x02
\(em so the two may not be combined.
.IP
Of the 132 portable John labels, 96 are indexable and 36 are refused for
the ordinary reason, a salt or a userid they cannot supply.  Only the
portable table is consulted: John's config-defined dynamics are numbered
per machine, so selecting through them would mean one
.B \-H
line naming different types on two hosts.  With no
.B \-H
the default six are indexed.  Fewer types means a proportionally smaller
index; see
.B Hash types
above for what may be selected and why.  The set is read back from the
manifest by
.BR update ,
so a type can only be added by a full
.BR build .
.IP
.B mdxfind
iterates by repeating a type's
.I outermost
call over the previous round's digest, carrying any case transform with it,
rather than by nesting the whole expression.  The two readings agree for a
single-call type and differ for every chain, so a chain that carries
something other than its printed output declares its step in
.BR hx (8),
and
.B mdindex
builds the declared form.  A type with no single outermost call and no
declared step is indexed at depth 1 only, and a type that enumerates its
own salt domain is not iterated at all, its
.B mdxfind
arm having a loop of its own.
.TP
.BI \-i " depth"
Index iterations 1 to
.I depth
of every selected type, as
.BR mdxfind 's
.B \-i
does.  Build only; the default is 1.
.B mdxfind
iterates a type by applying its OUTERMOST function again to the previous
round's output, re-rendered as hex in that type's own case convention, so
iteration 2 of
.I md5(sha1(pass))
is
.I md5(md5(sha1(pass)))
and not
.IR md5(sha1(md5(sha1(pass)))) .
A type whose chain carries something other than its printed output declares
its own step and is iterated by that instead.  A type with no single
outermost call has no step to repeat and is indexed at depth 1 only; one
that enumerates its own salt domain has its own loop and is not iterated.
.IP
Depth is counted in the
.B xNN
of the type name, not from the type's own first iteration: a chain whose
first real round is
.B x02
yields
.B x02
and
.B x03
at
.BR "\-i 3" .
Each extra depth costs another record per word per type.
.TP
.B \-y
Proceed past the refusal described under
.B Build-cost projection
above.  The projection is still printed.
.TP
.B \-V
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-v
Progress during
.B build
and
.BR update ,
and a per-phase timing breakdown during
.B lookup
\(em sort, index probe or scan, fetch and verify, and the number of digests
regenerated.
.IP
Progress is a percentage, in the three phases that can run long: the
per-source scatter, the 256-partition emit, and an
.BR update 's
probe of the index, which is one random seek per new word and the slow
half of a nightly run.
.IP
There is
.I no
progress indicator during
.BR lookup ,
by design and not by omission.  Its standard output carries solutions and
is meant to be piped; its standard error carries the counts that go with
them.  A progress line in the middle of that is noise to a pipeline that
has no use for it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/mdfind
Default database directory, overridden by
.BR \-d .
.TP
.I manifest
Tab-separated text, and the only human-readable file in the database: a
version line, the hash-type list, a row per hx type carrying its
expression and any guard, enumerated domain, extra output shape or
suppressed label, a
.B hxuser
row naming the
.B \-C
file and
.B id
behind each user-defined type, then one row per source file giving index,
bytes consumed, size, mtime, line count and the head and tail
fingerprints.
.IP
The expressions are here rather than looked up by name because the
expression
.I is
the type: a database whose manifest recorded only
.B SHA1MD5PASSx01
could be re-opened by a build whose catalog had since changed that
expression, with nothing to notice.  This file, not the catalog and not
any
.B \-C
file, is what an existing index means.
.TP
.I index.dir
Bucket directory, 16,777,217 big-endian 64-bit offsets into
.IR index.rec ,
one per bucket plus a terminator.
.TP
.I index.rec
The records, 15 bytes each, sorted by key: 9 bytes of digest residue then a
48-bit locator packing an 11-bit file index and a 37-bit offset.  The other
24 bits of the 96-bit key are implied by the bucket.  Anything that reads
this file must take the record width from the same source the writer does:
a reader carrying its own copy of the constant reports every bucket
unsorted the first time the format widens, which looks exactly like total
corruption and is not.
.TP
.I add.rec
The overlay: records added since the last
.B build
or
.BR compact ,
18 bytes each \(em the whole 96-bit key, then the same 48-bit locator \(em
sorted, binary-searched, and with no directory of its own.  Written by
.BR update ,
removed by
.BR compact ,
and searched by every lookup.  A record here is one the main index does not
hold, so the two files never both answer for the same key.
.TP
.I part/
Scratch partitions, present only while a build or update is running and
removed on success.  A
.I part
directory left behind means the run died; it is safe to delete.  Scratch
peaks at about 108 bytes per source line with all six types: one 18-byte
record per line per type, written before dedup.
.PP
Everything on disk is big-endian, so the database is portable between
hosts, and between platforms: a database built on one runs on another
unchanged.  Limits are 2,048 source files and 137 GB per file.
.PP
.B mdindex
builds on macOS, Linux, FreeBSD and Windows, 64-bit only.  A 32-bit build
is not offered: the tool exists to address files past the 4 GB a 32-bit
offset reaches, and
.I index.dir
alone is 134 MB before a single record is written.
.PP
A single line may be up to 262,144 bytes.  A longer one is
.B dropped
\(em not indexed, and not fatal, because one malformed record in a four
billion line source must not end a multi-hour build.  Dropped lines are
counted and the total is printed per source file, which matters: a word that
was skipped cannot be told at lookup time from one the index never held, so
the count is the only evidence it happened.  The decoded form of a
.BI $HEX[ ... ]
line is capped at half that, 131,136 bytes, which is what a line of full
length denotes.  A
.BI $TESTVEC[ HH " x " N ]
expands independently of the length of the line that writes it, so it does
not share that buffer: it expands into its own, capped at 2,097,152 bytes,
the same limit
.BR mdxfind (1)
and
.BR hashpipe (1)
apply, and the buffer is allocated once per worker and only if a vector is
actually met.  A vector over that limit is refused rather than truncated,
because a shortened vector hashes to a well-formed digest for a word that
was never presented.
.PP
Expect
.I index.rec
to be
.I larger
than the wordlists it indexes.  A record is 15 bytes whatever the word
costs, and one source line leaves about 3.6 of them once duplicate keys are
dropped \(em six types, and up to three NTLM readings of a non-ASCII word
\(em so a line averaging 13 bytes of text occupies some 55 bytes of index.
These three sources total 53.6 GB and their index is 226 GB.  The ratio is a
property of the wordlist rather than of the index: it falls as the average
line gets longer, and
.B \-H
with fewer types lowers it proportionally.
.PP
The database is worth excluding from backups \(em it is large and entirely
derived.  On macOS:
.PP
.RS
.nf
asroot tmutil addexclusion \-p /usr/local/share/mdfind
touch /usr/local/share/mdfind/.metadata_never_index
.fi
.RE
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Success, including a lookup that found nothing.  An absent hash is an answer,
not an error.
.TP
.B 1
Fatal: a source file is missing or has been rewritten, the database is
unreadable or of the wrong version, or an argument is wrong.
.TP
.B 2
At least one candidate failed its re-hash check, or
.B check
found a bucket out of order or a duplicate key.  The index no longer matches
a source file, or no longer matches itself; rebuild.
.SH EXAMPLES
Build over three files, highest-priority first:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ mdindex \-v build hashmob.found hibp.pass solved.words
.fi
.RE
.PP
Look a word up.  No command word is needed, and the count and timing go to
standard error:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ printf password | md5 | mdindex
MD5x01 5f4dcc3b5aa765d61d8327deb882cf99:password
1 hit for 1 of 1 hashes, lookup time 0.000s, 0.000126s per hash
.fi
.RE
.PP
Half an MD5 is enough, and the full digest comes back:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ mdindex 5f4dcc3b5aa765d6
MD5x01 5f4dcc3b5aa765d61d8327deb882cf99:password
.fi
.RE
.PP
Six characters match across every indexed type:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ mdindex 5f4dcc
MD5x01 5f4dcc3b5aa765d61d8327deb882cf99:password
MD5x02 5f4dcc93ef0450ccf4627a4fd62464b5:4hqgms*
NTLMx01 5f4dcceae810ddf7f9115b012e5020af:chevyhemi2
NTLMx01 5f4dcc5ea2cbee4d41a4fff4374c491f:Bluebaby7
13 hits for 1 of 1 hashes
.fi
.RE
.PP
A whole hash file, with the phase breakdown:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ mdindex \-v < hashes.txt > recovered.txt
2,000 hits for 2,000 of 2,000 hashes, lookup time 18.472s, 0.009236s per hash (108.27 lookups/sec)
  sort              0.000s  2,000  5.57M/s
  index probe      18.108s  2,000  110.45/s
  fetch+verify      0.363s  2,000  5.51K/s
  regenerated               12,000 hash computations
.fi
.RE
.PP
The probe is the whole cost.  Each one is a random read of a 226 GB file and
runs at the speed of the device, not of the CPU; the fetches that follow are
cheap because the candidate locators are sorted before any line is read.
Past the batch size given under
.B lookup
the probes are replaced by one sequential pass and the line reads
.I index scan
instead.
.PP
Check the overlay after an update, which is the only part an update can have
changed:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ mdindex check overlay
/usr/local/share/mdfind/add.rec: 920 records, 0 out of order, 0 duplicate keys
.fi
.RE
.PP
Fold the overlay back into the index.  This rewrites
.I index.rec
in full and is never required for correctness:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ mdindex \-v compact
.fi
.RE
.PP
Pick up lines appended to a source, and add a new wordlist at the lowest
priority:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ mdindex update
$ mdindex update /path/to/newlist.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Resolve what a run failed to solve.
.BR mdsplit (1)
leaves the unsolved hashes in
.IR hashes.txt.left :
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ mdindex < hashes.txt.left | hashpipe
.fi
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR mdxfind (1),
.BR hashpipe (1),
.BR mdsplit (1),
.BR rehex (1),
.BR rling (1),
.BR md5 (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Waffle.
